# Entity-First References

Home Assistant devices get replaced when they fail. Entity-based configuration survives hardware swaps — users re-point inputs to the new entity instead of reconfiguring a whole device graph.

## Blueprint Inputs

Use `entity` selectors for anything that triggers or acts on a specific thing:

```yaml
# ✅ GOOD — survives device replacement
door_sensor:
  selector:
    entity:
      domain: binary_sensor
      device_class: door

# ❌ BAD — breaks when the physical device is swapped
door_sensor:
  selector:
    device:
      integration: zwave_js
```

Prefer `entity_id: !input my_entity` in triggers, conditions, and actions — not device IDs or device-scoped selectors.

## When Device Selectors Are Acceptable

Use `device` selectors only when the blueprint genuinely needs device-level behavior that cannot be expressed per-entity (rare). If you reach for a device selector, document why entities are insufficient.

## Naming and Documentation

- Name inputs `*_entity` (e.g. `light_entity`, `motion_sensor`, `media_player_entity`)
- In specs and READMEs, refer to "the motion sensor entity" not "the motion sensor device"
- When setup docs mention finding entities, point users to the entity list on the device page — not the device ID itself

## Migration Benefit

After replacing hardware, the user updates one entity input. Device-based blueprints force re-import or full reconfiguration because device registry entries do not carry over.
